Title: Pattern Matching — Koka Feature Guide
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Pattern Matching { .heading-no-number }

Pattern matching in Koka lets you deconstruct values and branch on their shape in a single, concise expression. It works on all algebraic data types — enumerations, structs, recursive types, and tuples — and the compiler warns you about non-exhaustive matches at compile time.

---

## Basic `match`

Use `match` to inspect a value and bind its parts:

```koka
type color
  Red
  Green
  Blue

fun describe(c : color) : string
  match c
    Red   -> "red"
    Green -> "green"
    Blue  -> "blue"
```

Each branch is a _pattern_ followed by `->` and a result expression.

---

## Matching with Data

Constructors that carry data can bind their fields in the pattern:

```koka
type number
  Infinity
  Integer( i : int )

fun show-number( n : number ) : string
  match n
    Infinity   -> "infinity"
    Integer(i) -> "integer: " ++ i.show
```

Unused fields are matched with `_` (wildcard):

```koka
fun head-of-list(xs : list<int>) : string
  match xs
    Nil       -> "empty"
    Cons(x,_) -> "first: " ++ x.show
```

---

## Guards

Branches can include a boolean _guard_ after `|`:

```koka
fun classify(n : int) : string
  match n
    i | i < 0  -> "negative"
    0           -> "zero"
    i | i < 10 -> "small"
    _           -> "large"
```

If the guard fails, matching continues to the next branch.

---

## `as` Bindings: Naming a Subpattern

Use `name as pattern` to bind a name to a matched value _and_ simultaneously test an inner pattern.
The `name` is bound to the sub-expression; the `pattern` must also match.

The key use case is when you need **both a subvalue and its contents** in the same branch.
For example, to get the second element of a list while also keeping the tail:

```koka
fun drop-and-keep-tail(xs : list<int>) : maybe<(int, list<int>)>
  match xs
    Cons(_, rest as Cons(x, _)) -> Just((x, rest))
    _ -> Nothing
```

Here `rest as Cons(x, _)` binds `rest` to the whole tail, while simultaneously checking that
the tail is non-empty and binding `x` to its head. Both names are available in the branch body.

Without `as`, you'd have to match twice or reconstruct the list:

```koka
fun drop-and-keep-tail2(xs : list<int>) : maybe<(int, list<int>)>
  match xs
    Cons(_, Cons(x, rest)) -> Just((x, Cons(x, rest)))  // have to reconstruct!
    _ -> Nothing
```

---

## Named Field Patterns

When a constructor has named fields, you can match by field name using `field = pattern`
inside the constructor argument list. Unmentioned fields are implicitly wildcarded:

```koka
struct person
  age  : int
  name : string

fun is-adult(p : person) : bool
  match p
    Person(age = a) | a >= 18 -> True
    _                          -> False

fun greet(p : person) : string
  match p
    Person(name = n, age = a) -> "Hello " ++ n ++ ", age " ++ a.show
```

Named field patterns avoid having to match positionally — you only name the fields you need.

---

## Nested Patterns

Patterns can nest arbitrarily deep:

```koka
fun sum-pairs(xs : list<(int,int)>) : int
  match xs
    Nil             -> 0
    Cons((a,b), rest) -> a + b + sum-pairs(rest)
```

---

## Matching Structs

Named struct fields can be matched positionally, or you can use dot access after binding:

```koka
struct point { x : int; y : int }

fun on-origin(p : point) : bool
  match p
    Point(0, 0) -> True
    _           -> False
```

---

## Exhaustiveness

Koka checks that all possible constructors are covered. If you miss one, the compiler warns you. This helps catch bugs when you add a new constructor to a type later.

---

## `match` as an Expression

`match` is an expression and can appear anywhere:

```koka
fun max-list(xs : list<int>) : maybe<int>
  match xs
    Nil        -> Nothing
    Cons(x,Nil) -> Just(x)
    Cons(x,rest) -> Just(max(x, max-list(rest).default(x)))
```

